{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 260,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are currently hurtling through time at\nthe speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3860,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But be careful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4860,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If even a tiny bit of your breakneck temporal\nvelocity leaks into one of the dimensions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9080,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10080,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you’re standing in the wrong place at\nthe time, you will rapidly accelerate to your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13850,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14850,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You think I’m kidding?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15850,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I just described the true source of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19530,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t look down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25250,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Clocks run slow in gravitational fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27630,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our GPS satellites tick faster by a factor\nof 1-in-a-billion - enough to thrown their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32320,
    "dDurationMs": 2089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position accuracy off by 11km per day."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34409,
    "dDurationMs": 7891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our recent episode, we saw why this gravitational time dilation is inevitable - it follows as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42300,
    "dDurationMs": 6939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surely as 1+1=2 if we accept the two axioms of Einstein’s relativity theory: that the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49239,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of light is constant for all observers,\nand that the weight induced by acceleration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55179,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is fundamentally the same as that induced\nby gravity - the so-called equivalence principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61269,
    "dDurationMs": 3881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But none of this is very satisfying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65150,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that gravity must cause clocks to\nrun slow on the basis of logical consistency."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71800,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we know that gravity DOES cause clocks\nto run slow based on many brilliant experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77630,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I never explained WHY or HOW gravity causes\nthe flow of time to slow down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83500,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And I’m not going to explain it now - because\nin a sense it’s not true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88520,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravity does NOT warp the flow of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90770,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the other way around - the warping\nof time causes gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95130,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s what I’m going to show you right\nnow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97890,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you didn’t watch the previous episode\n- do it, though it’s also ok if you watch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102470,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this one first."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103650,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just don’t forget."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105170,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how is it that time causes gravity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108690,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with … a teapot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110600,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I lA nice china teapot hanging in space, minding\nits own business."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116140,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Absent a gravitational field or any forces,\nif the teapot starts motionless it stays that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121380,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122380,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least, it stays motionless with respect\nto the three dimensions of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127380,
    "dDurationMs": 2449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Everything is moving through the dimension\nof time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129829,
    "dDurationMs": 3201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can show this with our old friend the spacetime\ndiagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133030,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s have just two dimensions of space\nand so we have space … for time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138090,
    "dDurationMs": 3799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We show progression through time as the object\nmoves up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141889,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You could say that it has a positive velocity\nthrough time, and zero velocity through space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147579,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, now let’s add a second object - something\nnice and massive … the planet Earth will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152909,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153909,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that the presence of mass and energy\nwarp spacetime - and the most intense part"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158590,
    "dDurationMs": 4119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that warping is in time - our gravitational\ntime dilation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162709,
    "dDurationMs": 3631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things closer to the Earth move through time\nmore slowly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166340,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can show this as a bunch of identical clocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169030,
    "dDurationMs": 2249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They tick as they move up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171279,
    "dDurationMs": 4341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Clocks closer to the Earth take longer to\ntick for every tick on a distant clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175620,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Velocity through time increases away from\nthe Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178120,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we move particles through time according\nto those velocities, we have this sense of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182389,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time flowing in a gradient - faster streams\ndistant from the Earth, slower streams near"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187999,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188999,
    "dDurationMs": 4561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kind of like how water in the center of a\nstream flows more quickly than the edge, where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193560,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the shallower stream drags on that flow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196610,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s almost like Earth’s mass creates\na drag on the flow of time around it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202260,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what happens to an object sitting in this\nstream of time - parts further away from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207909,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth age faster, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209849,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, yes, but that’s not all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212439,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can think of any object as being made of\nmany tiny clocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216999,
    "dDurationMs": 4231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each atom, each subatomic particle trying\nto tick at its own rate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221230,
    "dDurationMs": 4149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And each of those clocks has a velocity vector\nin time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225379,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what’s the temporal velocity of the entire\nobject?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229569,
    "dDurationMs": 4261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Einstein’s relativity you have to remember\nthat time and space are not independent of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233830,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234830,
    "dDurationMs": 3999,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Objects don’t just have a velocity through\nspace or through time - they have a velocity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238829,
    "dDurationMs": 1601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240430,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call this their 4-velocity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242810,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get at this, let’s move away from our\nteapot for a second and talk about boats."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248359,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine two boats on an actual stream."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252049,
    "dDurationMs": 3981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One near the edge moves slow and one near\nthe center moves fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256030,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The slow boater reaches out an oar which the\nfast boater grabs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261260,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happens?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262750,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instinct tells us that the fast boat is pulled\ntow ards the shore."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266760,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can think of the two objects as becoming\none object, and the difference in velocities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270760,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across its length causes a torque that rotates\nthe overall velocity vector towards the shore.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276700,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the same with the 4-velocity of an\nobject in a gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281160,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The gradient of velocities cause the overall\n4-velocity of the object to be rotated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287050,
    "dDurationMs": 8270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All individual 4-velocities start out being\npurely in time, but the sum is rotated partially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295320,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296750,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’s always rotated in the direction\nof decreasing flow - which in a gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301680,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field is downwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303550,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this is the motion of any object in a gravitational\nfield - it gradually picks up velocity in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308670,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the down direction - it accelerates - and\nit pays for that acceleration by losing velocity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313460,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- Decelerating in the time direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315980,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's a certain way of interpreting the\nmath of relativity that says that everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320570,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travels at the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322910,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light travels at the speed of light through\nspace - obviously enough - and we know that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327590,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing with mass can reach that speed traveling\nthrough space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331540,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we interpret time as a dimension like\nspace, then a stationary mass really is moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336930,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the fastest possible speed in the temporal\ndirection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340760,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is something we can come back to another\ntime - for now let's go with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344320,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The 4-velocity of a massive object is pointed\nalmost entirely in the time direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350090,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, light itself travels at\nthe speed of light through space only, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354590,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not at all through time - a photon’s clock\nis frozen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357710,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might imagine it’s 4-velocity is entirely\nrotated out of the time direction into space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362690,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- although technically photons and other massless\nparticles don’t have a 4-velocity, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367170,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is defined according to the ticking of your\nown clock - your proper time - which is zero"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372690,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the timeless photon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373850,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this picture, a falling object trades some\nof its enormous velocity through time to pay"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378700,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for a small velocity through space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381090,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To us currency exchange looks favorable for\nspace - a teapot gains a rapid plummet to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386170,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its doom for an imperceptible slowing of its\nclock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389480,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see the same favorable exchange when we\ntry to convert mass into energy via Einstein’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394480,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most famous equation, E=mc^2 - the speed of\nlight is the exchange rate, and the speed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400210,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of light is very large."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402640,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, two of my favorite physics channels\nhave great, slightly different explanations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407300,
    "dDurationMs": 1210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408510,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Check out Nick Lucid on Science Asylum and\nEugene Khutoryansky’s channel to get their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413660,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "takes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415280,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is that it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416650,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do we now perfectly understand the source\nof gravity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419730,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well speak for yourself - I’m still confused."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423300,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This raises thorny questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425220,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like - what about a particle with no size\n- supposedly point-like particles like electrons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430610,
    "dDurationMs": 1270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quarks, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, actually, nothing truly occupies only\na single, perfectly defined position in space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437400,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- quantum uncertainty means that everything\nis always at multiple places at once, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442450,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so experiences the gradient of time flow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445700,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually general relativity doesn’t\nneed quantum mechanics to explain gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449930,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s enough to imagine clocks that are infinitesimally\nseparated and we still have our time gradient."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455920,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other thorny question is about light itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459110,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If photons are already fully rotated into\nthe spatial direction, how is it that they’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463780,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also affected by gravitational fields?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465690,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They have no “velocity through time” to\ntrade."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468950,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But light DOES bend in a gravitational field\n- astronomers see it happening all the time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473780,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the effect we call gravitational lensing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476180,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, the imaginary paths of light rays\nwere one the most important tools that helped"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481420,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein develop both special and general\nrelativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484590,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’d better understand the effect of\ngravity on the path of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488980,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To do so we’re going to need to shift our\nperspective in a couple of mind-bendy ways"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493280,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see how the flow of time determines the\npath of even timeless particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498030,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And with those new perspectives we’ll get\na new insight into the source of gravity that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503240,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems weirdly at odds with everything I just\ntold you - and yet is simultaneously exactly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508000,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as correct."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509360,
    "dDurationMs": 3799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But first you’re going to need to some time\nto think on everything I’ve just told you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513159,
    "dDurationMs": 5421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and let it settle, and we’re going to need\nsome time to make that new episode of space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518580,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519580,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last time we talked about the gravitational\nwave background - the ambient buzz of gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524180,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves from the distant and ancient universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526420,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which, by the way, we may have detected using\na pulsar timing array."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531970,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ivan Kilmoc asks where he can find the audio\nfiles of the pulsars that we played in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537910,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538910,
    "dDurationMs": 1990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sorry, I should have linked those in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540900,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll link them in the description for this\nvideo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544200,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re from a number of different radio\ntelescopes, but were collated by the Parkes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548800,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Observatory in Australia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550900,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stu Lora asks if I can elaborate on my comment\n“the time before the big bang” - which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556410,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mentioned in reference to a potential component\nof the gravitational wave background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560370,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I was referring to the extremely energetic\nevents during the inflationary epoch - fluctuations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566870,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the so-called inflaton field, or in the\nfinal decay of those inflatons at the end"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572390,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of inflation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573390,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I call this “before the big bang” because\nmany physicists are moving away from the picture"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578760,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where you have the big bang, then you have\nan instant of inflation, then regular expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584570,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, in the eternal inflation model,\ninflation may have lasted for a very very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589080,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long time and still be continuing almost everywhere\n- but it ceased in isolated bubbles - corresponding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595010,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the formation of a new universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597120,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It makes more sense to talk about the end\nof inflation as the beginning of such a universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601800,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than the beginning of global inflation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604950,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in that case the last instant of inflation\nIS the instant of the big bang, and gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613779,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kinkusnacht asks whether gravitational waves\ncan be used to test ideas in quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619889,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is absolutely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621809,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most well known prospect is by detecting\nthe signatures of primordial gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626019,
    "dDurationMs": 2609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves - waves from the inflationary epoch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628628,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These could be found in the gravitational\nwave background, but also indirectly through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632759,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their effect on the cosmic microwave background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635959,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Interaction of those waves with matter right\nafter inflation may have caused characteristic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640399,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "patterns in the distribution of matter, which\nwe might now see in the way the CMB light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645289,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is polarized."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646889,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The BICEP2 experiment claimed detection of\nthese so-called “b-modes” but it turns"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651459,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out they were wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652539,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the b-modes may be there, and we’re\ndigging deeper to find them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656878,
    "dDurationMs": 3281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "During inflation it’s believed that quantum\ngravitational effects would have been very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660159,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "important, so if we can get any type of signal\nfrom then perhaps we can learn something."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666119,
    "dDurationMs": 7069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No Mercy8008 says that the way I described\nthe boats rocking on the ocean suggests that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673188,
    "dDurationMs": 2571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I would be an awesome dungeon master."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675759,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hm."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676759,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'd say I'm at best an ok dungeon master."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678959,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think I just rolled a nat 20 on my skill\ncheck to describe boats rocking on the ocean."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683499,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or did you fumble your saving through versus\nbeing impressed by someone describing boats"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686979,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rocking on the ocean?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688239,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, thanks for the compliment and for\nreminding me how much I miss the game."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692099,
    "dDurationMs": 740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So... who wants to play?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
